--TEST--
`instanceof` through a subject whose tag nothing proved — a `mixed`, a plain `object`, a `?Class` tested and untested — walks the concrete class, and every non-object tag answers `false` rather than throwing — ADR 0007 § 2, ADR 0007 § 7 row 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Ask {
    // `bool as string` renders `false` as the empty string, so a row of
    // predicates read through it silently loses its false columns.
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }
}

interface Named {
    public function name(): string;
}

class Animal implements Named {
    public function name(): string {
        return "animal";
    }
}

final class Dog extends Animal {
    public function name(): string {
        return "dog";
    }
}

// ADR 0007 § 2's one unchecked position: nothing proved this holds an object
// at all, which is precisely the question `instanceof` is being asked.
mixed $erased = new Dog();
echo Ask::yn($erased instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($erased instanceof Animal),
    Ask::yn($erased instanceof Named),
    "\n";

// The same three questions through a plain `object` and through a nullable
// class, tested and untested — the answers agree, because every one of them
// walks the *concrete* class behind the handle and none of them reads the
// declared type. A subject shape that grew its own answer fails here while
// still looking right on its own line.
object $opaque = new Dog();
?Animal $untested = new Dog();
?Animal $narrowed = new Dog();
echo Ask::yn($opaque instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($opaque instanceof Animal),
    Ask::yn($opaque instanceof Named),
    "\n";
echo Ask::yn($untested instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($untested instanceof Animal),
    Ask::yn($untested instanceof Named),
    "\n";
if ($narrowed != null) {
    echo Ask::yn($narrowed instanceof Dog),
        Ask::yn($narrowed instanceof Animal),
        Ask::yn($narrowed instanceof Named),
        "\n";
}

// The test still walks up and never sideways or down through an erased
// subject: a base instance is not its own subclass.
mixed $base = new Animal();
echo Ask::yn($base instanceof Animal), Ask::yn($base instanceof Dog), "\n";

// Every tag a `mixed` can carry that is not an object answers `false` — the
// answer PHP gives, and the reason this is a run-time question rather than the
// `E0497` a *declared* scalar takes where it is written.
mixed $num = 7;
mixed $ratio = 1.5;
mixed $flag = true;
mixed $txt = "dog";
mixed $arr = ["k" => "v"];
mixed $nil = null;
echo Ask::yn($num instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($ratio instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($flag instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($txt instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($arr instanceof Dog),
    Ask::yn($nil instanceof Dog),
    "\n";

// The nullable half of the same rule: a `?Class` holding `null` is not an
// instance of anything, and asking costs no test.
?Animal $missing = null;
echo Ask::yn($missing instanceof Animal), Ask::yn($missing instanceof Named), "\n";

// A subject nothing names is only read, so the test releases it once it has
// answered — a fresh object here used to outlive the expression that built it,
// which `tools/leak-check.sh` is what pins.
echo Ask::yn((new Dog()) instanceof Named), Ask::yn((new Animal()) instanceof Dog), "\n";

// A method call through the concrete class the test just proved, so the row
// above is answering about a live object rather than a stale handle.
echo $erased instanceof Named ? "named" : "anonymous", "\n";
--EXPECT--
yyy
yyy
yyy
yyy
yn
nnnnnn
nn
yn
named
